Surfaced while fixing karr #126. ADR 0011 declares engine-native stage keys "unreserved by the framework" and read per-engine. k126 is the first proof that they are NOT namespaced and can collide semantically:

  Engine::Ollama  timing.total_duration = NANOSECONDS
  Engine::AKI     timing.total_duration = SECONDS (before k126)

Any consumer reading the raw hashref -- which ADR 0011 explicitly sanctions for stage keys -- was wrong by 1e9 depending on which engine answered.

k126 fixed the code side: Engine::AKI now emits the engine-agnostic total_seconds (ADR 0011 standard key) plus a native compute_seconds, and no longer emits a raw total_duration key at all. That ends this specific collision.

ADR-worthy amendment (adr-auditor lane): record that engine-native timing keys share one flat namespace, so a native key that duplicates another engine's spelling with different units is a semantic collision the framework does not catch. Options to weigh: (a) document "prefer the engine-agnostic *_seconds accessors; native keys carry no unit guarantee across engines"; (b) reserve unit-suffixed conventions (_seconds vs _duration=ns) as house rule; (c) namespace native keys by engine. Amend ADR 0011 Consequences accordingly.

Done by langertha-adr-auditor 2026-09-01 (uncommitted in the working tree; orchestrator commits).

DECISION: amendment IN PLACE in ADR 0011, not a new ADR that supersedes it.
Reason: none of ADR 0011's four shape decisions changed -- one timing HashRef, two key classes,
first-write-wins, metaclass clone_with all stand. Only one sentence of Decision 1 is narrowed
and a hazard is recorded. The house precedent for minting a new number is ADR 0006 -> 0013,
where the placement axis of the thing itself moved (and 0006 got a "## Update (ADR 0013)"
back-pointer section). Nothing moved here.

