OPTION CHOSEN: (b) unit-bearing suffix convention, with (a) as consumer-facing guidance.
  The _seconds suffix is now reserved and unit-bearing: any key ending in _seconds is a Float
  in seconds, engine-agnostic and engine-native alike, and an engine reporting a stage timing
  emits it under that suffix, converting if its wire uses another unit. *_duration is NOT a
  parallel convention -- it is Engine::Ollama's back-compat legacy in nanoseconds, kept because
  it shipped, and no new engine adds one. Engine::AKI (b4da770) is the first engine written
  under the rule (total_seconds + compute_seconds, no raw total_duration).
  Narrowing: no engine-native key STEM is reserved; the _seconds SUFFIX is. Native keys still
  share one flat namespace, so two engines may still land on the same stem -- but under the
  suffix rule such a collision is unit-compatible, the survivable kind.
  (c) rejected: namespacing would break the shipped Ollama keys and the flat HashRef[Num]
  type, force _merge_timing_field and the Langfuse anchor to learn a nesting level, and buy
  nothing the suffix rule does not already give.

